  "articleBody": "Installing crown molding can elevate a room, but there are a few common missteps that can quickly turn it into a headache. One of the biggest culprits is inaccurate measurements. Seriously, measure twice, cut once! Another frequent issue is cutting the wrong spring angle or miter direction, which leads to frustrating gaps and wasted material. Don't forget to account for out-of-square corners; pretending they're perfect will only lead to visible gaps. Poor fastening into the framing is another pitfall, as it can cause the molding to sag or pull away over time. And finally, visible gaps from not scribing or caulking properly can really detract from an otherwise good job. To avoid these problems, take your time with measurements and test cuts. Invest in a good miter saw and understand how to cut crown molding at an angle. Always fasten into studs or blocking for a secure hold. And for a truly professional finish, master the art of caulking and filling. If all this sounds a bit much, especially for large rooms or complex profiles, bringing in a seasoned professional is definitely worth considering. They can save you a lot of frustration and ensure a flawless result.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
